{
  "about": "How each thing that happens feels, on every device: parts/feel plays these rows; this file is all of them. A screen says what happened with sayHappened({ event_kind, ... }) (parts/ui/show/happened); a moment row answers an event the way records/delight-moments.json rows do (on.event_kind, on.match, on.starts_with) and names a feel. A feel says what each output plays: `haptic` (events as Core Haptics describes them: at seconds, intensity and sharpness 0 to 1, an optional duration for a held buzz; `vibrate` is the same feel as milliseconds for phones that only vibrate), `sound` (tones synthesised on the device: at seconds, hz, an optional hz_to it glides to, ms, gain, wave) and `motion` (Web Animations keyframes and options played on the thing that was pressed, sent or dropped; `reduced` replaces it when the device asks for less motion). A feel without an output plays nothing there. Whether an output plays now is the person's show rules (subject feel, facts moment, feel and output, with the hour and device of the moment), and a rule that changes things can give any moment another feel. Adding a moment, a feel or an output is a row here; sounds share one key (A major pentatonic) so they never clash. Research behind the numbers: Apple's Playing haptics, Core Haptics AHAP, Duolingo and Things (agent-junk/2026-10-05/touch-feel/research.md).",
  "repeat_gap_ms": 1200,
  "outputs": [
    { "id": "haptic", "label": "Touch" },
    { "id": "sound", "label": "Sound" },
    { "id": "motion", "label": "Motion" }
  ],
